From f88a63bb88766f55f4dac7a13f2fd05ba88de397 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 13:00:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Process a scanned card in batches, and keep what is not a
 number
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Uploading the 33 MB, ~100-page health card failed with 413 request_too_large.
The whole PDF went into one Messages API call, and that call is capped at 32 MB
per request — base64 adds a third on top, so a 33 MB scan arrives as ~44 MB —
and at 100 pages, which a full card reaches on its own. Pages now go in batches
of ten, three requests at a time, and the replies are merged: metrics as parsed
data rather than pasted arrays, so one batch answered in an unexpected shape
cannot corrupt the array for the others.

The quieter half of the problem is why "aké som mal operácie" could not be
answered no matter how often the card was uploaded. The extraction prompt asked
for a JSON array of lab values and nothing else, so the operations, diagnoses,
medication and doctors' conclusions on those pages were never produced in the
first place — there was no text to store, and the RAG work that came before
this was indexing the metrics JSON. The prompt now returns two sections: a
written record of everything that is not a measured value, and the metrics.
The record is what gets indexed; only the JSON reaches the extractor, because
it reads from the first '[' to the last ']' and a reference range printed as
[3.5-5.5] in a report would otherwise swallow the real array.

max_tokens goes to 8192 — the reply now carries the record as well.

Note on cost: transcription is extra output tokens on every page, and this runs
on claude-opus-4-5. A hundred-page card is a real charge. The model choice
predates this change and is left alone, but ai-model-selection-strategy.md
already argues this task belongs on a cheaper model with Opus as escalation.

